                {
                  "type": "concept",
                  "title": "Feedback Loops: Negative and Positive",
                  "content": "The endocrine system maintains homeostasis primarily through **negative feedback loops** — a hormone's output ultimately suppresses its own further release. This is the same engineering principle used by a household thermostat.\n\n**Classic negative feedback example — the HPT axis:**\n1. Hypothalamus releases **TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone)**.\n2. TRH stimulates anterior pituitary to release **TSH**.\n3. TSH stimulates thyroid to release **T3 and T4**.\n4. Rising blood T3/T4 **inhibits TRH at the hypothalamus and TSH at the pituitary**.\n5. As stimulation falls, T3/T4 drops, the inhibition lifts, and the loop restarts.\n\nThis is the **three-level feedback architecture** common to TRH-TSH-thyroid, CRH-ACTH-cortisol, and GnRH-FSH/LH-gonads. The set point can be modulated by stress, circadian inputs, or developmental state, but the loop always tracks toward it.\n\n**Positive feedback** is rare and reserved for situations that need to be driven to completion. Two endocrine examples:\n• **Oxytocin during labor** — uterine stretch triggers oxytocin release; oxytocin causes more contraction; contraction stretches more, releasing more oxytocin. Resolves with delivery.\n• **LH surge before ovulation** — rising estrogen flips from negative to positive feedback on LH at midcycle, triggering the LH spike that causes ovulation.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">FEEDBACK LOOPS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">How hormones regulate themselves</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"170\" width=\"1000\" height=\"440\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">NEGATIVE FEEDBACK</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Output suppresses input - stable set point</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"325\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Example - HPT axis:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"365\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">1. Hypothalamus releases TRH</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"405\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">2. Pituitary releases TSH</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"445\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">3. Thyroid releases T3 and T4</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"485\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">4. T3/T4 inhibits TRH and TSH</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"525\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">5. Levels drop - loop restarts</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"575\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Same architecture as HPA (cortisol) and HPG (sex)</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"630\" width=\"1000\" height=\"380\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"685\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">POSITIVE FEEDBACK (rare)</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"735\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Output amplifies input - drives to completion</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"785\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">OXYTOCIN in labor:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"825\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">- uterine stretch triggers oxytocin</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"865\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">- oxytocin causes more contraction - more stretch</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"915\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">LH SURGE before ovulation:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"955\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">- estrogen flips to positive midcycle</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"995\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">- triggers LH spike - ovulation</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "Negative for stability; positive for big one-off events"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "title": "Blood Glucose Regulation: Insulin vs Glucagon",
                  "content": "Plasma glucose is normally maintained between **70 and 100 mg/dL** fasting, despite irregular meals, exercise, and overnight fasts. Two pancreatic hormones make this possible by acting as direct antagonists.\n\n**After a meal (postprandial — glucose rises)**:\n1. Plasma glucose climbs above 100 mg/dL.\n2. Pancreatic **β cells** sense the rise via glucose entering through GLUT2 and secrete **insulin**.\n3. Insulin acts on muscle and adipose tissue → **GLUT4 vesicles insert** into the plasma membrane, allowing glucose uptake.\n4. Insulin acts on the liver → glucose is stored as **glycogen** (glycogenesis) and excess as **triglyceride** (lipogenesis).\n5. Plasma glucose returns to baseline; insulin secretion falls.\n\n**Between meals or during fasting (glucose falls)**:\n1. Plasma glucose drops below 80 mg/dL.\n2. Pancreatic **α cells** secrete **glucagon**.\n3. Glucagon acts on the liver via GPCR/cAMP/PKA → **glycogen broken down to glucose** (glycogenolysis), and amino acids/lactate converted to glucose (gluconeogenesis).\n4. Glucose is released into the bloodstream.\n5. Plasma glucose returns to baseline; glucagon falls.\n\nThis insulin-glucagon antagonism is the textbook **negative feedback loop** of an endocrine variable held between two opposing effectors — the same architectural pattern seen in PTH vs calcitonin (Ca²⁺) and ADH vs ANP (water).",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">BLOOD GLUCOSE LOOP</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Insulin vs Glucagon - antagonistic control</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"170\" width=\"500\" height=\"430\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">GLUCOSE HIGH (postprandial)</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Glucose above 100 mg/dL</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"325\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Beta cells release</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"360\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">INSULIN</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"410\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Muscle/fat insert GLUT4</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"445\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">- glucose uptake</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"495\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Liver makes glycogen</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"530\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">and triglyceride</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"575\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Glucose drops to baseline</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"170\" width=\"480\" height=\"430\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">GLUCOSE LOW (fasting)</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Glucose below 80 mg/dL</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"325\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Alpha cells release</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"360\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">GLUCAGON</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"410\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Liver breaks glycogen</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"445\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">(glycogenolysis)</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"495\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Liver makes new glucose</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"530\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">(gluconeogenesis)</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"575\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Glucose rises to baseline</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"620\" width=\"1000\" height=\"390\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"675\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">NORMAL RANGE 70-100 mg/dL FASTING</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"730\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Antagonistic pairs - core endocrine motif:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"780\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Glucose - insulin vs glucagon</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"820\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Calcium - calcitonin vs PTH</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"860\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Water - ANP vs ADH/aldosterone</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"920\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">One hormone raises, the other lowers</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"960\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Variable held by net balance</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "Antagonistic hormone pairs are the universal endocrine pattern"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "quiz",
                  "question": "A patient's plasma calcium drops below normal. Which response correctly describes the homeostatic correction?",
                  "options": [
                    {
                      "text": "Thyroid C cells release calcitonin, which stimulates osteoclasts to release Ca²⁺ from bone.",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Parathyroid chief cells release PTH, which stimulates osteoclasts, increases renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption, and activates vitamin D for gut absorption.",
                      "correct": true
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Adrenal zona glomerulosa releases aldosterone, which increases calcium reabsorption in the proximal tubule.",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pancreatic delta cells release somatostatin, which increases gut absorption of calcium.",
                      "correct": false
                    }
                  ],
                  "explanation": "Low plasma Ca²⁺ triggers **parathyroid chief cells** to release **PTH**. PTH executes a three-pronged response: (1) stimulates **osteoclasts** to resorb bone and release Ca²⁺; (2) increases renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption in the **distal convoluted tubule**; (3) stimulates renal conversion of inactive vitamin D to **calcitriol**, which increases gut Ca²⁺ absorption. Option 1 is wrong because calcitonin **lowers** Ca²⁺ (the opposite hormone) and **inhibits** osteoclasts. Aldosterone regulates Na⁺/K⁺, not Ca²⁺. Somatostatin is a paracrine inhibitor with no role in Ca²⁺ homeostasis.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Calcium homeostasis</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"170\" width=\"1000\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">STIMULUS</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Plasma Ca below normal (8.5 mg/dL)</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"315\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Detected by parathyroid chief cells</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"390\" width=\"1000\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"445\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">PTH RESPONSE - 3 ACTIONS</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"495\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">1. Bone - stimulate osteoclasts</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"535\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">2. Kidney - reabsorb Ca in DCT</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"575\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">3. Kidney - activate vitamin D</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"620\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">    - calcitriol then drives</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"660\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">    - gut Ca absorption</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"730\" width=\"1000\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"785\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">WHY NOT?</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"835\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Calcitonin LOWERS Ca - wrong direction</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"880\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Aldosterone is Na/K, not Ca</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"925\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Somatostatin is a paracrine inhibitor</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"970\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">PTH and calcitonin form the Ca antagonistic pair</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "PTH = the master calcium-raising hormone"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "application",
                  "title": "Why the Endocrine System Matters",
                  "content": "The endocrine system is the body's **long-term homeostatic controller**. Every other organ system depends on it.\n\n• **Cardiovascular** — ADH and aldosterone set blood volume; epinephrine sets cardiac output during stress; ANP from atrial myocytes counterregulates.\n• **Urinary** — kidney is both an endocrine organ (renin, EPO, calcitriol) and the principal target of aldosterone, ADH, ANP, and PTH.\n• **Musculoskeletal** — bone is remodeled by PTH and calcitonin; muscle responds to insulin, IGF-1, cortisol, thyroid hormone, testosterone.\n• **Nervous** — hypothalamus is the bridge; cortisol modulates stress; thyroid hormone is required for normal CNS development.\n• **Digestive** — gastrin, secretin, CCK, GIP, and ghrelin coordinate digestion and appetite; pancreatic insulin processes the absorbed glucose.\n• **Reproductive** — entirely run by the HPG axis (GnRH → FSH/LH → sex steroids).\n\nThe defining feature is **chemical specificity by receptor**: hormones reach every cell in the body, but only target cells with matching receptors respond. This is how a single signal molecule like cortisol can drive dozens of distinct tissue-specific programs simultaneously — from gluconeogenesis in the liver to immunosuppression in lymphoid tissue.\n\nWhen a single endocrine loop fails, downstream cascades fail with it. Hashimoto's thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes mellitus, Cushing syndrome, Addison disease, and panhypopituitarism are all examples of broken feedback loops with multisystem consequences.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">INTEGRATION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Endocrine connects every system</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"170\" width=\"500\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">CARDIOVASCULAR</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">ADH + aldosterone</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"310\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Epi at stress, ANP balance</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"170\" width=\"480\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">URINARY</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Renin, EPO, calcitriol</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"310\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Target of ADH, PTH, aldo</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"370\" width=\"500\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"425\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">MUSCULOSKELETAL</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"475\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">PTH + calcitonin on bone</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"510\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Insulin, IGF-1, T3 on muscle</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"370\" width=\"480\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"425\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">NERVOUS</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"475\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Hypothalamus is bridge</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"510\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">T3 for CNS development</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"570\" width=\"500\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"625\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">DIGESTIVE</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"675\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Gastrin, secretin, CCK</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"710\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Insulin for absorbed glucose</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"570\" width=\"480\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"625\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">REPRODUCTIVE</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"675\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Entire HPG axis</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"710\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">GnRH - FSH/LH - sex steroids</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"770\" width=\"1000\" height=\"240\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"825\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">THE BIG IDEA</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"875\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Hormones reach every cell</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"915\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">but only cells with receptors respond</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"965\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">One molecule - tissue-specific programs</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "Endocrine is the bridge that keeps every system in balance"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "quote",
                  "title": "The Big Idea",
                  "content": "Two communication systems run the body: the nervous system fires fast and stops; the endocrine system whispers in chemistry and lasts. Together they make integrated physiology possible — every cell hears the conversation, but only the cells with the right receptor answer back. Mastering endocrinology means mastering the chemical grammar by which the brain talks to the body, day after day, hour after hour, breath after breath.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE BIG IDEA</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Endocrine system in one sentence</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"200\" width=\"1000\" height=\"500\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"280\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">Every cell in the body hears</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"325\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">the endocrine conversation,</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"395\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">but only the cells with the right</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"440\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">receptor answer back.</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"525\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">The brain talks to the body</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"570\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">in chemistry, hour after hour,</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"615\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">breath after breath - and the</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"660\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"22\">body listens.</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"780\" width=\"900\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"845\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">FROM THIS POINT FORWARD</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"895\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Watch hormones appear in</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"930\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">every system we study next</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"1010\" width=\"900\" height=\"70\" rx=\"35\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"1055\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Receptors are destiny</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "Closing the chemical loop on Unit 4"
                  }
                }
